    "content": "An IAN Alert is a Kentucky-based emergency notification system designed to find missing children under 18 who have autism, intellectual disabilities, or related conditions, often activating when a child has wandered or eloped. Named after Ian Sousis, this alert functions similarly to an AMBER Alert to quickly inform the public and improve safe recovery chances. Key Details About IAN Alerts: Purpose: To quickly mobilize the public when a vulnerable child with autism or mental illness goes missing, particularly when they may not meet the strict abduction criteria of an AMBER Alert. Activation: The alert can be issued for children under 18 with autism or similar developmental needs. Significance: It was developed following the 2024 death of 9-year-old Ian Sousis, who ran away and drowned. Effectiveness: In March 2026, the system successfully helped locate a missing teenager in Kentucky within 45 minutes. Background and Context The Kentucky State Police (.gov) issues these alerts to notify the public of missing children who may be in danger. The system was created as a, Spectrum News 1 reported, specialized tool to help \"her grandson can still be a superhero,\" according to his grandmother, Rhonda O’Brien. It often highlights the risk of children with autism wandering, specifically mentioning that many are drawn to water.",
